{"name":"Coquette","hex":"#e5dcdc","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"coquette","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A woman who flirts or plays with people's affections.","Any hummingbird in the genus Lophornis."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Alternative form of coquet."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/coquette"}],"entries":[{"title":"Coquette aesthetic","blocks":[{"text":"Coquette aesthetic is a 2020s Gen Z fashion trend that is characterized by a mix of sweet, romantic, and sometimes playful elements that focus on femininity through the use of clothes with lace, flounces, pastel colors, and bows. It often draws inspiration from historical periods, such as the Victorian era and the 1950s–'60s, with a modern twist."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Marie_Antoinette%2C_from_Leaders_series_%28N222%29_issued_by_Kinney_Bros._MET_DPB872310.jpg/330px-Marie_Antoinette%2C_from_Leaders_series_%28N222%29_issued_by_Kinney_Bros._MET_DPB872310.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coquette_aesthetic","wikidata":"Q124632328"},{"title":"The Coquette (film)","blocks":[{"text":"The Coquette is a 1917 German silent comedy film directed by Franz Eckstein and Rosa Porten and starring Porten, Reinhold Schünzel, and Eduard von Winterstein. It premiered at the Marmorhaus in Berlin."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/9/9e/The_Coquette_%281917_film%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Coquette_(film)","wikidata":"Q60738614"},{"title":"Coquette (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Coquette is a 1929 American pre-Code drama film directed by Sam Taylor and starring Mary Pickford, who won the second Academy Award for Best Actress for her performance."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Coquettemp.jpg/330px-Coquettemp.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coquette_(film)","wikidata":"Q570506"},{"title":"Coquette (play)","blocks":[{"text":"Coquette is a 1927 play by George Abbott and Ann Preston Bridgers. It is a three-act tragedy, with a single setting and eleven speaking characters. The action of the play takes place over seven months time. The story concerns a smalltown belle of good family who falls in love with an unsuitable young man."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Best_Plays_1927-28-179.jpg/330px-Best_Plays_1927-28-179.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coquette_(play)","wikidata":"Q140306728"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Coquette_lobby_card.jpg/500px-Coquette_lobby_card.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Coquette_lobby_card.jpg/500px-Coquette_lobby_card.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Lobby card","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACoquette_lobby_card.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Mary_Pickford_posing_with_statuette_at_the_Oscars_1930.jpg/500px-Mary_Pickford_posing_with_statuette_at_the_Oscars_1930.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Mary_Pickford_posing_with_statuette_at_the_Oscars_1930.jpg/1280px-Mary_Pickford_posing_with_statuette_at_the_Oscars_1930.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Mary_Pickford_posing_with_statuette_at_the_Oscars_1930.jpg/500px-Mary_Pickford_posing_with_statuette_at_the_Oscars_1930.jpg?utm_source=en.wik